$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: safekeys/system
$type: system
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-28"
version: "0.1.0"
status: planned
summary: |
  Safekeys.ai — encrypted transport for secrets through environments, agentic flows, and LLM systems, designed so plaintext is never exposed to any model. Secrets are treated as movable encrypted objects (ciphertext folders) plus opaque capability tokens; a privileged local sidecar resolves them outside the LLM process. The agent-facing interface is stable as the backend hardens from software isolation to hardware and threshold cryptography.

identity:
  name: Safekeys
  domain: safekeys.ai
  contact: james@smith-gray.com
  repository: https://github.com/Smith-Gray-Pty-Ltd/safekeys

principles:
  - key: never-on-decrypt-path
    name: The LLM is never on the decryption path
    statement: No language model ever holds, receives, or can derive plaintext secret material or long-term keys.
    rationale: Once an LLM has seen a value, that session — and the logs, traces, and memory around it — is compromised. This is the product's reason to exist.
    implications:
      - Agents may only receive tokens, folder paths, ciphertext, metadata, and success/deny status
      - Resolution happens in a privileged sidecar, never in the model process
      - Create-secret flows never round-trip plaintext through model context
  - key: opaque-capability
    name: Agents see only opaque capability tokens
    statement: The only secret-bearing artefact an agent reasons about is a short-lived, scoped, audience-bound capability token containing zero secret material.
    rationale: A token is safe to pass through prompts, memory, tool calls, and multi-agent buses where a value is not.
    implications:
      - Tokens are short TTL (minutes to hours), scoped, and audience-bound
      - Revocation is immediate via jti denylist plus short TTL
  - key: stable-agent-interface
    name: Stable agent-facing interface
    statement: The public interface (tokens plus folders) stays constant as backends harden from software isolation to hardware to threshold crypto.
    rationale: Agent frameworks, runtimes, and customers integrate once and inherit later hardening without rewrites.
    implications:
      - MVP is software-only and already meets the product promise
      - Hardware, threshold crypto, and post-quantum ratchets land behind the same API
  - key: materialise-then-zeroise
    name: Plaintext is materialised only for the consumer, then zeroised
    statement: Plaintext exists only transiently inside the sidecar's injection path for the consuming non-LLM process, and is zeroised immediately after use.
    rationale: Minimises the window and the surface where a live secret value exists.
    implications:
      - Prefer exec-wrapper and env injection over returning values on an agent's stdout
      - Sidecar holds transient unwrapped data keys, never long-term master keys in the hardware phase
  - key: open-core-protocol
    name: Open protocol, commercial authority
    statement: Token and folder specifications, the sidecar reference implementation, CLI, and core SDKs are open source; the hosted authority, hardware, and enterprise controls are commercial.
    rationale: Security tooling requiring deep trust wins adoption when the foundational pieces are auditable — the Let's Encrypt model — while revenue sits on hosted authority, hardware, and enterprise controls.
    implications:
      - Public core is Apache-2.0 (explicit patent grant matters for crypto)
      - Control plane and hardware provisioning remain proprietary or BSL
      - No AGPL/SSPL on the sidecar or SDKs — it scares agent-framework maintainers

roles:
  - name: Agent Developer
    description: Engineers building agents who need secrets in tool calls and runtimes without exposing them to the model.
    needs:
      - Token-only SDKs and MCP tools they can adopt without bespoke crypto
      - Clear integration path (MCP, CLI exec, Python SDK)
  - name: Security Engineer
    description: The person who must approve Safekeys for production and reason about the threat model.
    needs:
      - Auditable trust boundaries and the open protocol specification
      - Explicit statement of what is and is not mitigated at each phase
  - name: Platform / SRE Operator
    description: Runs the sidecar fleet, control plane, and key store in the customer's environments.
    needs:
      - Install and infrastructure requirements, key store integration
      - Audit log access and revocation tooling
  - name: AI Coding Agent
    description: An automated agent working in this repository, which must consult specs before changing code.
    needs:
      - Machine-readable contracts and flows per feature
      - A stable system map to locate the governing spec

services:
  - id: control-plane
    name: Control Plane API
    ref: apps/control-plane
    port: 8080
    type: api
    depends_on:
      - key-store
      - control-plane-db
  - id: sidecar
    name: Sidecar / Resolver
    ref: apps/sidecar
    port: 0
    type: worker
    depends_on:
      - control-plane
      - key-store
  - id: cli
    name: Safekeys CLI
    ref: apps/cli
    port: 0
    type: api
    depends_on:
      - sidecar
      - control-plane
  - id: safekeys
    name: Safekeys.ai Site
    ref: apps/site
    port: 3000
    type: web-app
    depends_on: []
  - id: mcp-server
    name: Safekeys MCP Server
    ref: packages/mcp-server
    port: 0
    type: shared
    depends_on:
      - cli
  - id: python-sdk
    name: Python SDK
    ref: packages/sdk-python
    port: 0
    type: shared
    depends_on:
      - cli
  - id: typescript-sdk
    name: TypeScript SDK
    ref: packages/sdk-typescript
    port: 0
    type: shared
    depends_on:
      - cli
  - id: key-store
    name: Key Store (OpenBao / Vault)
    ref: https://openbao.org
    type: external
    depends_on: []
  - id: control-plane-db
    name: Control Plane Database
    ref: apps/control-plane/internal/store
    port: 5432
    type: database
    depends_on: []

apis:
  - id: control-plane-api
    name: Safekeys Control Plane API
    ref: apps/control-plane

auth_schemes:
  - id: control-plane-api-key
    type: api-key
    description: Client credential used by the sidecar and CLI to authenticate to the control plane in the MVP.
    config_ref: docs/help/control-plane-auth.md
  - id: sidecar-mtls
    type: oauth2
    description: Mutual TLS device identity between sidecar and control plane, introduced alongside hardware binding.
    config_ref: docs/help/control-plane-auth.md
  - id: local-sidecar-socket
    type: none
    description: Local authenticated channel (Unix domain socket) between an agent/CLI and the sidecar on the same host.

infrastructure:
  cloud: cloudflare (docs and marketing site); customer-hosted or hosted control plane for the product itself
  region: global edge
  terraform_ref: ""
  dns: safekeys.ai
  ssl: cloudflare-managed

deployment:
  environments:
    - name: dev
      url: http://localhost:8080
      type: local
      notes: Control plane and sidecar via `make dev`.
    - name: docs
      url: https://docs.safekeys.ai
      type: cloudflare-pages
      build_command: make generate && usm docs build --audience help
      output_directory: .usm-workspace/help-docs/.vitepress/dist
      project_name: safekeys-docs
      secrets:
        - CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
        - CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
      notes: |
        Help docs (VitePress, filtered for the public audience). Deployed by
        .github/workflows/docs-deploy.yml on push to main. Cloudflare Pages — free tier.
    - name: dev-docs
      url: https://dev-docs.safekeys.ai
      type: cloudflare-pages
      build_command: make generate && usm docs build
      output_directory: .usm-workspace/docs/.vitepress/dist
      project_name: safekeys-dev-docs
      secrets:
        - CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
        - CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
      notes: |
        Contributor docs (VitePress, developer audience — architecture, design, references).
        Deployed by .github/workflows/docs-deploy.yml on push to main. Cloudflare Pages — free tier.
    - name: site
      url: https://safekeys.ai
      type: cloudflare-pages (planned)
      notes: |
        Marketing site. apps/site is not built yet; see safekeys/docs-hosting.
  notes: |
    MVP operator footprint: 2–3 small VMs or one small Kubernetes namespace, managed Postgres,
    OpenBao/Vault on a hardened VM, API keys plus mTLS for sidecars. Low hundreds of USD per month
    at early stage. Customer install is a single sidecar binary or container plus a control-plane URL
    and client credential.

    The docs and marketing site are static and hosted on Cloudflare Pages, which is independent of
    the product's own infrastructure above.

local_development:
  container_runtime: >-
    OrbStack, not Docker Desktop. The Docker context is "orbstack" and the Compose project name is
    "safekeys", derived from the repository directory (docker-compose.yml declares no explicit
    `name:`). Containers are therefore named safekeys-postgres-1 and safekeys-openbao-1, the network
    is safekeys_default, and the volume is safekeys_pgdata. If `docker context show` does not print
    "orbstack", the wrong daemon is in use and image builds will be slow or fail.
  external_services:
    - name: postgres
      port: 5432
      purpose: Control-plane state — objects, tokens, policy, and the append-only audit log.
      managed_by: docker compose (project "safekeys") on OrbStack — docker-compose.yml
    - name: openbao
      port: 8200
      purpose: >-
        Key store holding the KEK. Local development runs it with `server -dev` and a fixed root
        token, which is explicitly not a production configuration; production uses
        deploy/openbao/openbao.hcl instead.
      managed_by: docker compose (project "safekeys") on OrbStack — docker-compose.yml
  apps:
    - name: control-plane
      port: 8080
      dev_command: make dev
      url_local: http://localhost:8080
      requires_db: true
    - name: sidecar
      dev_command: make dev
      requires_db: false
    - name: safekeys-site
      port: 3000
      dev_command: npm run dev --workspace apps/site
      url_local: http://localhost:3000
      requires_db: false
  environment:
    root_env: >-
      .dev/ — generated by scripts/dev-up.sh and git-ignored. Holds the stable dev signing key, the
      local KEK, and the ciphertext folders so tokens survive a restart.
    required_vars:
      - "SAFEKEYS_SOCKET — defaults to $TMPDIR/safekeys/sidecar.sock; the Go, TypeScript and Python clients all default to this same path"
      - "SAFEKEYS_API_KEY — defaults to dev-api-key"
      - "SAFEKEYS_ALLOW_INSECURE_KEYSTORE=1 — development only; enables the local file KEK"
  log_locations:
    dev_server: .dev/control-plane.log and .dev/sidecar.log (written by make dev)
    build_output: bin/ (make build)
    test_output: stdout from go test; .dev/ holds the two-agent demo's artifacts
  known_quirks:
    - id: make-dev-and-make-demo-socket-disagreement
      title: make demo could not find the sidecar socket make dev had bound
      description: >-
        scripts/dev-up.sh binds the conventional $TMPDIR/safekeys/sidecar.sock so the Go, TypeScript
        and Python clients discover it with no configuration, but scripts/demo.sh and
        scripts/dev-down.sh still defaulted to .dev/sidecar.sock. `make demo` therefore failed with
        "no sidecar at .dev/sidecar.sock" unless SAFEKEYS_SOCKET was already exported.
      workaround: Export SAFEKEYS_SOCKET to the path dev-up.sh reports before running the demo.
      affected_command: make demo
      fixed_in: unreleased — demo.sh and dev-down.sh now derive the same $TMPDIR path as dev-up.sh
    - id: host-overload-is-not-safekeys
      title: High host CPU load does not come from the Safekeys stack
      description: >-
        This machine also runs the smith-gray-ai project (11 containers: langfuse, litellm, zitadel,
        postgres, redis, minio) alongside safekeys (2) and daingerdainger (1). The Safekeys stack is
        near-idle — postgres and openbao each use around 1% CPU and under 60MB. Observed load
        averages of 20+/50+/100+ are driven by the other stacks and by OrbStack's own helper, not by
        Safekeys, so a slow `docker build` or test run should not be attributed to this project.
      workaround: >-
        Check `docker stats --no-stream` before blaming Safekeys. Use `make dev-down` to stop the
        Safekeys stack; the smith-gray-ai and daingerdainger stacks are separate projects.
      affected_command: docker build, make images
      fixed_in: n/a — an environmental condition, recorded so it is not misdiagnosed

operations:
  monitoring: Prometheus metrics from the sidecar and control plane — resolution latency and deny rates.
  alerts: Deny-rate spikes and key-store reachability.
  on_call: Single team in MVP.

security_stack:
  first_line: Capability token verification — signature, expiry, audience, and policy checks at the sidecar.
  last_line: Envelope encryption with a key store holding wrapped keys; plaintext never persisted.
  notes: |
    The threat model is explicit about what is deferred: a fully compromised secure element plus all
    threshold shares, or a human pasting plaintext into a chat, remain out of scope even after Phase 3.

testing_strategy:
  automated_testing: Per-service unit and integration tests, plus a two-agent demo test asserting neither transcript contains plaintext.
  security_testing: Prompt-injection and stolen-token abuse cases asserted against the sidecar.
  ci_integration: usm generate --check plus per-service lint, typecheck, and test on the affected modules.

non_functional:
  performance:
    target: Resolution and injection under 50ms p95 locally, excluding key-store unwrap latency.
    description: The sidecar sits on the hot path for tool calls, so resolution must stay cheap.
  scalability:
    target: Single sidecar per decrypting environment; control plane scales horizontally behind Postgres.
  security:
    target: Compromise of the model, the agent sandbox, or the sidecar must not yield the long-term key.
    description: Architectural in the MVP; cryptographic plus hardware in later phases.
  reliability:
    target: Deny-by-default when the key store or control plane is unreachable.
    description: A failed resolution must never fall back to plaintext or a cached value.
  maintainability:
    target: Agent-facing API unchanged across software, hardware, and threshold phases.

